# Cloudflare static-asset response headers.
#
# Nothing was cached before this file existed: every asset shipped
# `cache-control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`, so a reader moving
# between two docs pages revalidated 22 assets (54 on a page with diagrams),
# each one a round trip that returned an empty 304. That also defeats Astro's
# hover prefetch, which only helps if the prefetched bytes are still there when
# the click lands.
#
# The paths below match what `pnpm build` actually emits into dist/.
# `scripts/check-seo.mjs` parses this file and asserts it against dist/.

# Security headers, on every response. None were sent before.
#
# HSTS has no `includeSubDomains` and no `preload`: other hosts under
# queenmq.com may not serve HTTPS, and preload is close to irreversible. It
# only takes effect once a browser has reached the site over HTTPS; the
# HTTP -> HTTPS redirect itself is the zone's "Always Use HTTPS" setting, not
# something a static-asset Worker can answer.
#
# The CSP allows nothing from another origin: every script, style, font and
# image is served from here, and the only requests a page makes (prefetch,
# "Copy page", the Pagefind index) are same-origin.
#   script-src   No 'unsafe-inline'. The three inline scripts the build emits
#                are allowed by hash: the theme bootstrap (every page), the
#                desktop sidebar's group state (docs pages) and the homepage
#                header's scroll state. Change one and its hash changes;
#                check-seo.mjs fails the build with the new hash to put here.
#                'wasm-unsafe-eval' is for Pagefind, which compiles its search
#                index reader from WebAssembly in a worker.
#   style-src    'unsafe-inline' because pages carry `style=""` attributes
#                (headings, figures) and Mermaid writes inline styles.
#   font-src     data: because the header stylesheet inlines a small woff2.
#   frame-ancestors 'none' and X-Frame-Options: DENY say the same thing to new
#                and old browsers: no page here is meant to be framed.
/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' 'sha256-dqcmwrHMBG7uo7u3L4cMDzwG8wr8naMTg+aYi2R8MSQ=' 'sha256-WLkmCQrDNilcsSDm7EAuRm/E0DqN0suNkO/hh57hMco=' 'sha256-CPDS92rsGe0EmMcU8ohRhwg22wRrXo/Ym51cJHCsVEk='; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'; worker-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests

# Content-hashed by the bundler: the filename changes when the bytes change,
# so the response never has to be revalidated.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Not content-hashed. A week is short enough that a regenerated figure, social
# card or search index reaches readers on its own, and long enough to cover a
# reading session.
/charts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/og/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/pagefind/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/screenshots/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

# Every page has a markdown twin at `<page>/index.md`. The twins carry the same
# text as the HTML and cannot declare a canonical of their own, so left
# indexable they double the crawlable surface with pure duplicates.
#
# `llms.txt` and `llms-full.txt` are deliberately NOT listed here. They are the
# entry points we want AI crawlers taking freely, and neither duplicates a
# single ranking page.
# Both forms are listed on purpose. The splat is documented as greedy across
# `/`, which makes `/*.md` enough on its own, but every twin this rule exists
# for lives two or more segments deep (`/use/streams/index.md`). If the splat
# ever stops crossing a segment boundary the first rule silently matches
# nothing and the whole duplicate surface goes back to being indexable, so the
# explicit form is kept as insurance. Same header either way, and overlapping
# rules merge, so a request that matches both is unaffected.
/*.md
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

/*/index.md
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

# The same for the authored-source twins at `<page>/index.mdx`
# (src/pages/[...slug]/index.mdx.ts), which the `.md` twins link to. They were
# missed when the two rules above were written and stayed indexable.
/*.mdx
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

/*/index.mdx
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

/404
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
